This is a working sheet on whether a combinatorial pseudo-line configuration can be drawn with straight lines (stretchability). Many labels are hard to read, so the incidences below are a best reading.

(1) The two top sketches each show a square, normalised with corners (-1,1) and (1,1) visible, so side 2. Several lines cross it, and dashed lines mark where the lines meet the sides.

(2) The main figure turns this into a coordinate frame:
- two horizontal lines: the upper one P–L–B–C'–N and the lower one N'–C–B'–L'–P';
- two vertical lines: the left one through L, A', N', R' and the right one R–N–A–B'–M;
- slanted lines, roughly: the heavy line Q–Q'–A'–C–M; the heavy line M'–A–L'; the line through C' rising from R'; the long faint line P–A'–A–P'; and further faint lines.

The segments are labelled:
- a = LB (or the stretch near B) and b = BC', on the upper horizontal;
- a' = AB', on the right vertical;
- b' = CB', on the lower horizontal.

(3) Because the frame has width/height 2, the required order of the crossing points along each line becomes linear inequalities in these lengths:
- a + a' ≤ 2;
- b + b' ≥ 2;
- 2 − a' < b, i.e. a' + b > 2.

From these he concludes b > a, since a ≤ 2 − a' < b. This is the usual test of whether an arrangement given by local orders can be realised by straight lines, by deriving metric inequalities that are compatible or contradictory.

(4) Context: the previous page has allowable-sequence-like data (q1…q6 with numbers) and the next page shows an arrangement D0…D5 with hatched cells. So this page is a realisability check of a small (about 6-line) arrangement inside Grothendieck's study of pseudo-line systems.
